		{
			"id": "112917195043034278",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@pawelgrzybek/112917194697430840",
			"author": {
				"name": "Paweł Grzybek",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@pawelgrzybek",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/306/993/798/153/333/original/37ce2e77cc184610.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-06T21:27:35.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> this was a very interesting read Zach.</p><p>At the company that I work for, we received a surprisingly high bill from Vercel and after looking closely into the usage breakdown we noticed that the number of used cache units is so so so high. Too high to be even possible taking into consideration our revalidation interval and number of pages. Unfortunately Vercel CS is not able to give us a detailed breakdown of where we spent these cache units 🤷‍♂️</p><p>PS. I love the term „convenience tax” !</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112917204450544388",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://bensie.social/@james/112917204377838822",
			"author": {
				"name": "James Miller",
				"url": "https://bensie.social/@james",
