		{
			"id": "112922668810479861",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli/112922668479837917",
			"author": {
				"name": "Pelle Wessman",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/288/782/009/996/943/original/7631cc5a4e9dde08.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-07T20:39:38.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Serverless functions are essentially the good old PHP CGI model adapted to the cloud and given a hype name.</p><p>Just like PHP struggles with idle threads during async I/O, so does serverless functions.</p><p>Depending on which serverless function provider one uses it ranges from being autoscaled worker with a strict one payload at a time processing (and no prefetch of next item) to being fresh executions for each and every request.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112922681160869845",
			"parentId": "112922668810479861",
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli/112922681047429863",
			"author": {
				"name": "Pelle Wessman",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/288/782/009/996/943/original/7631cc5a4e9dde08.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-07T20:42:50.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> Whenever possible I would opt to run workers myself and work on dialing in how many simultaneous payloads and prefetched ones they should have to maximize throughput, latency and cost</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112922688578711120",
