		{
			"id": "112922695304141661",
			"parentId": "112922681160869845",
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli/112922695262750101",
			"author": {
				"name": "Pelle Wessman",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/288/782/009/996/943/original/7631cc5a4e9dde08.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-07T20:46:27.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I remember reading up on that theory when I worked with RabbitMQ some 5-10 years ago, something like this: <a href=\"https://www.rabbitmq.com/blog/2012/05/11/some-queuing-theory-throughput-latency-and-bandwidth\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://www.</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">rabbitmq.com/blog/2012/05/11/s</span><span class=\"invisible\">ome-queuing-theory-throughput-latency-and-bandwidth</span></a></p><p>Also get reminded of good old <a href=\"https://gearman.org/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"\">gearman.org/</span><span class=\"invisible\"></span></a> which we eg used with our PHP setup on Flattr back in the days and could handle extreme spikes in activity without any spikes in cost – only spikes in queue lengths</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112922698926811323",
