		{
			"id": "112924718505196598",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@munawwar/112924718439323752",
			"author": {
				"name": "Munawwar Firoz",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@munawwar",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-08T05:20:58.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> You can skip api gateway cost and save with lambda function URLs</p><p>(and to prevent \"denial of wallet\" attack, possibly set concurrency limit)</p><p>Other than that I've written few optimization tips:</p><p><a href=\"https://codepasta.com/2024/04/19/optimizing-puppeteer-pdf-generation\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">codepasta.com/2024/04/19/optim</span><span class=\"invisible\">izing-puppeteer-pdf-generation</span></a></p><p>BTW GCP cloud run makes this so much more easier with a simple Dockerfile</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112927620868867302",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://toot.cafe/@reconbot/112927620704360049",
			"author": {
				"name": "Francis 🏴‍☠️ Gulotta",
				"url": "https://toot.cafe/@reconbot",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/313/376/052/418/189/original/b24ed2bdf8f16f68.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-08T17:39:03.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>&gt; Mind-boggling that the same service would use ~1276 GB-Hrs per month on Vercel and ~101 GB-Hrs on AWS. Mind-boggling that the same service would use ~1276 GB-Hrs per month on Vercel and ~101 GB-Hrs on AWS.</p><p>This is with all the caching in place, not the same workload hitting lambda, right?</p><p>In either case yes mind boggling, and I used to run the team that owned that.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112927637847486795",
			"parentId": "112922695304141661",
			"url": "https://toot.cafe/@reconbot/112927637718792977",
			"author": {
				"name": "Francis 🏴‍☠️ Gulotta",
				"url": "https://toot.cafe/@reconbot",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/313/376/052/418/189/original/b24ed2bdf8f16f68.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-08T17:43:23.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>voxpelli</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> yes and you get guaranteed resources per request, and price per request, and incredible scaling capabilities. It’s absolutely not appropriate for every workload, but can adapt quickly and be very cheap. And despite some companies best efforts to downplay it, it’s often pretty trivial to move to a server execution model when it makes sense.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112927650329161074",
			"parentId": "112927637847486795",
			"url": "https://toot.cafe/@reconbot/112927650164262619",
			"author": {
				"name": "Francis 🏴‍☠️ Gulotta",
				"url": "https://toot.cafe/@reconbot",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/313/376/052/418/189/original/b24ed2bdf8f16f68.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-08T17:46:33.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@voxpelli\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>voxpelli</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I used to work in publishing and we would occasionally get viral successes. While the primary content is incredibly cachable. There was quite a lot of features that weren’t. Lambda helped us slice our server costs 90%. While the operational cost was higher during peaks. We only paid for it during peaks. And had very simple ops model.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112927796670860616",
			"parentId": "112927620868867302",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/112927796670860616",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-08T18:23:48.693Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://toot.cafe/@reconbot\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>reconbot</span></a></span> yeah, with caching—71% cache misses on the Vercel side. ~85% cache *hits* from CloudFront.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
